<h2>The contract and the fall-out</h2>
<p>NHAI awarded the petitioner a short-term improvement and maintenance contract in 2005 for a stretch of national highway between Baharampore and Farakka. Work was completed in July 2006, with a Defect Liability Period running to January 2007. Matters soured within that period: NHAI encashed the performance bank guarantee and retained the security deposit, taking the position that notified defects remained uncured. The contractor\'s claims — extra work beyond scope, rain-damage repairs, the encashed guarantee, the deposit, goodwill and business losses, and interest under Sections 70 and 73 of the Contract Act — went to a three-member tribunal, which rejected them by a 2–1 majority.</p>

<h2>What the tribunal found, and why it stood</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>Joint Inspection Reports</strong><p>Contemporaneous joint records showed defects outstanding during the liability period. Against them, the contractor\'s unilateral letters claiming completion carried less weight — a comparative assessment squarely within the tribunal\'s province.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Third-party rectification</strong><p>The tribunal found the remedial work was ultimately done by another agency, supporting NHAI\'s recourse to the security.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Scope of work</strong><p>The claimed "extra work" fell within the contract\'s scope as the tribunal construed clauses 11.1, 13.1 and 33 — a construction of contract terms that is quintessentially the arbitrator\'s domain.</p></div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Conditions for refund</strong><p>Clause 44.3\'s preconditions for releasing the security deposit were unmet; clause 44.2 justified withholding the performance security.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The Section 34 standard, restated</h2>
<p>The judgment is a textbook application of the post-2015 scheme. Under Section 34(2A), a domestic award may be set aside for patent illegality appearing on its face, but not merely on the ground of an erroneous application of the law or by re-appreciation of evidence. The fundamental-policy ground in Section 34(2) is similarly confined. The Court found that the petitioner\'s grounds — that the inspection reports were wrongly preferred, that the extra work was proved, that the deposit ought to have been returned — were re-arguments of the merits dressed as challenges. Where two views are possible and the tribunal has taken one of them on the material before it, the Section 34 court\'s inquiry ends.</p>

<div class="compare">
<div class="col old"><h3>What a challenger must show</h3><p>Perversity in the sense of a finding with no evidence at all; patent illegality on the face of the award; breach of natural justice; an award beyond the submission to arbitration; conflict with the fundamental policy of Indian law.</p></div>
<div class="arrow">→</div>
<div class="col new"><h3>What will not suffice</h3><p>A plausible alternative reading of the evidence; a persuasive dissent; disagreement with the tribunal\'s construction of contract clauses; the sheer size of the rejected claim.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>Lessons for infrastructure contractors</h2>
<p>The dispositive documents in this case were created on site, not in court: the Joint Inspection Reports signed during the Defect Liability Period decided the arbitration and, through it, the Section 34 petition two decades after the contract was signed. Contractors should treat joint records with the seriousness of pleadings — attend inspections, record dissent on the spot, and follow up in writing contemporaneously, because unilateral correspondence written after the event rarely displaces a signed joint record. Equally, claims for "extra work" need written instructions or variation orders traceable to the contract\'s machinery; quantification without that foundation fails at the first finding of fact, and no court can revisit it under Section 34.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>The petition was dismissed and the majority award upheld in full. The decision does not alter the law on challenges to awards; its value lies in showing how firmly the re-appreciation bar holds even where the sums are large and the tribunal itself was divided.</p>
</div>
